<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Roussillon</h1><p class="st-meta">812 CE – 1172 CE (approx.) · capital Perpignan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Catalan county north of the Pyrenees centred on Perpignan, bequeathed to the Crown of Aragon in 1172 and French since 1659.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>, named after the ancient town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruscino">Ruscino</a>, became a Carolingian county in the early 9th century, part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marca-hispanica">Marca Hispanica</a>. It was long held with Empúries by a single comital family descended from Sunyer, until the two were divided in 991 and Roussillon got its own line of counts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>, which grew into a prosperous town on the route between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>. The economy rested on the fertile plain of the Têt, cloth making and trade. The county shared the Catalan language and laws, and monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sant-genis-de-fontanes">Sant Genís de Fontanes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sant-andreu-de-sureda">Sant Andreu de Sureda</a> flourished on its plain and foothills.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girard-ii-of-roussillon">Girard II</a>, died without heirs in 1172 and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon">Alfonso II of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. It later formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659 gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-roussillon.jpg" alt="County of Roussillon" width="320" height="344"><figcaption>Image: Каталонские_графства_в_IX-XII_вв.svg: Nekto derivative work: Lliura (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comtats_catalans_s._VIII-XII.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>812 CE – 1172 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Perpignan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gausfred II, Girard II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Catalan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rosselló, Comtat de Rosselló</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=992&amp;focus=county-of-roussillon" aria-label="County of Roussillon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4750 660 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Roussillon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5070 916L5139 672M5070 916L4984 945M5070 916L5065 1017M5070 916L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5139 672h0M4984 945h0M5065 1017h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5070 916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5070 916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Perpignan. <a href="/atlas?year=992&amp;focus=county-of-roussillon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
